<article class="env-remark" id="0CBU">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0CBU">Remark <span data-tag="0CBU">53.19.9</span> <span class="named">(Trivial quadratic extension)</span>.</a>  Let $k$ be a field. Let $(A, \mathfrak m, \kappa )$ be as in Remark <a href="/tag/0CBT" data-tag="0CBT">53.19.8</a> and let $\kappa '/\kappa $ be the associated separable algebra of degree $2$. Then the following are equivalent </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$\kappa ' \cong \kappa \times \kappa $ as $\kappa $-algebra, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
the form $q$ of Lemma <a href="/tag/0C49" data-tag="0C49">53.19.3</a> can be chosen to be $xy$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$A$ has two branches, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
the extension $A'/A$ of Lemma <a href="/tag/0C4A" data-tag="0C4A">53.19.4</a> has two maximal ideals, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$A^\wedge \cong \kappa [[x, y]]/(xy)$ as a $k$-algebra. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> The equivalence between these conditions has been shown in the proof of Lemma <a href="/tag/0C4A" data-tag="0C4A">53.19.4</a>. If $X$ is a locally Noetherian $k$-scheme and $x \in X$ is a point such that $\mathcal{O}_{X, x} = A$, then this means exactly that there are two points $x_1, x_2$ of the normalization $X^\nu $ lying over $x$ and that $\kappa (x) = \kappa (x_1) = \kappa (x_2)$. </p>
</article>